WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:02.910
Hello, I’m parenthetically Andrew Heaton

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.960
and you’re watching Mostly Weekly.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.779
And today we’re going to talk about patent
trolls.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.320
They’re like regular trolls, only balder,
paler,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.960
and prone to lawsuits.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.440
Patents are the legal equivalent of dibs.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.260
Or a temporary monopoly that the government
awards to inventors,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.820
so that after they’ve developed a new technology
they’re rewarded for their efforts.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.340
But patent trolls are gaming the system,

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.200
to the point that the patent system is holding
back innovation instead of encouraging it.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.460
Patent trolls buy or register stupid or vague
patents.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.140
Then when someone invents something awesome

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.240
they claim the inventor is using their patent,
and they sue.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.300
That creates a minefield where inventors are
afraid to create things

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.720
for fear they’ll step on a patent and blow
their legs off.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.020
Trolls cost US firms twenty-nine billion dollars
in lawsuits every year.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.200
And the threat of lawsuits strangles startups
and hampers innovation.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.700
Like this, or this, or this, it's good for
father's day.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.190
Or this, that's a good valentine's day present.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.940
Or this, Christmas?

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:07.100
To be awarded a patent, an inventor is supposed
to create a new idea

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.260
which is novel and not just an obvious improvement
over an existing idea.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.820
Like this!

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.900
The US Patent Office doesn’t nitpick every
moron’s application,

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:20.140
so patent trolls file broad, vague, and weak
applications

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.920
that they can use as flimsy pretenses to lob lawsuits
at people.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.080
For example, this is an actual patent:

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.140
it's patented as “thermal refreshening of
a bread product.”

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.520
Oh! You mean toast?

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.300
You can’t patent, friggin, toast!

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.580
Or this!

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.040
"Method of concealing partial baldness.”

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.260
That’s a combover!

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.240
You didn’t invent combovers!

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.700
If you think toast and combovers are overly
broad,

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.180
you’re right, you score a point-

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.380
which is also a patent!

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.420
Apple and Samsung had a patent dispute

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.220
over who owned rectangles with rounded edges.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:07.900
Quit fighting about quadrilaterals!

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.920
Just build me a robot butler already!

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.300
For years, trolls filed their frivolous lawsuits
in East Texas.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.800
Partly because East Texas was more friendly
to patent lawsuits,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.920
and also because it’s irritating to have
to fly to East Texas.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.780
So defendants, much like a substitute teacher
in her late thirties,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.880
are inclined to settle--

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.800
which is exactly what the trolls want.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.200
In the recent case of TC Heartland v. Kraft
Foods

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.220
the Supreme Court ruled that patent lawsuits
must be filed wherever the defendant is

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.420
Which is a big blow to patent trolls.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.510
Meanwhile, other patent troll victims have
started fighting back.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.900
Personal Audio LLC sued Adam Corolla for podcasting,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.040
which they claimed to own the patent for.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.069
Rather than ponying up extortion money,

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.480
Corolla raised half a million dollars through
podcasting

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.020
and threw in another 200,000 of his own to
fight them in court.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.280
We don’t know what the settlement was,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.180
but I imagine they didn't make very much money,

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.380
and they’ll probably think twice about trying
to extort the podcast community again.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.860
Which means I can now fearlessly release my
pogcast.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.120
Which is a podcast about pogs!

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.340
Hey, remember pogs?

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.720
Finally, a guy named Alexander Reben

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.680
developed an algorithm that spits out tens
of thousands of invention ideas every day.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.819
If a troll patents something in the future
and sues someone,

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.540
the defendant can point to the millions and
millions of Reben's ideas that predate it.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.480
And some of those ideas are brilliant.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.520
Like: “a shoe tree for one shoe”

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.600
and a pineapple lily plant that can track people.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:35.860
That's innovation!

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.820
Trolls are on the run, but there’s a major
step we can do take to hobble them.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.800
What do trolls fear most?

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.440
Commitment.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.160
Obviously, everyone knows that.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.520
But after that, “fee shifting.”

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.190
Fee shifting means whoever loses the court
case pays for all of the legal fees.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.020
It’s what they do in Canada and Britain,

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.880
although we only do it for exceptional cases
in the United States.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.959
If you knew you’d have to pay yours and
the defendant's fees

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:02.150
if you lost a frivolous patent case,

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.500
you’d be less likely to register it in the
first place.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.640
On my end, I'm going to file a patent for
“being an extortionist dickhead”

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.900
so I can sue the pants off some patent trolls.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.160
And that'll show them how the-

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.980
And apparently the patent for ironic patents
has already been patented so that's great.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.800
Now if you’ll excuse me, I have a court
date.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.460
But not for patent violations.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:24.580
For murder.

